Robert M. Riches Jr. wrote:
> Is there a known practical workaround:
> 
>   - changes to Mozilla code that sidestep the compiler bug?
> 
>   - modified HTML for the Yahoo sign-in page?
> 
>   - turning off javascript when signing in to Yahoo?
> 
> The rest of the thread seems to be about using a newer version of
> GCC to get around the compiler bug.  That is a bit beyond what I
> think I'm capable of doing successfully.

changing the Mozilla code would be a bit scary as that code gets hit a lot.

Any javascript that uses huge numbers (greater than 2^31) will trigger the 
problem.  Yahoo (and Excite) use Javascript to do md5sum, which does huge 
numbers.  Disabling Javascript will fix the problem as long as the page accepts 
a plaintext password in place of the more secure encryption.

current gcc (2.96-108.7.2) and gcc3 (3.0.4-1) exhibit this bug.  I have not 
tried 3.1

