Jozer <jozer@rcn.com> wrote:
> I am new to Linux.  What do I need to use x86 linux executables on my alpha
> with RedHat 7.2?

The real answer is as follows:

Get yourself a "hand-me-down" x86 box if you do not already own one.
You do not need for it a keyboard, mouse, video card and/or monitor.
You do need ethernet cards in both machines and a network connection (if
these are only two machines then a "null cable" will be enough).
Install Linux, with X support, and an ssh server.  From X screen on
Alpha logon via ssh to your x86 box and type a name of a program you
want to run.  It will just display on your screen on Alpha.  Throw in
'wine' into that and even some windoze programs will be usable. :-)

Even with a relatively slow x86 this is likely faster, less painful and
more "compatible" :-) than anything else.  There used to be em86 package
which provided some kind of emulation of x86 on Alpha.  The problem is
that it really required an "insider knowledge" to maintain it and this
ingredient is missing for a long time.  It was also used mostly to run
x86 Netscape on Linux/Alpha.  With Tru64-compat package you can run
Netscape from Tru64, and some other programs, and there are nowadays
better browsers for Linux/Alpha.  If you are thinking about something
tied very closely to x86 hardware that would have problems anyway and
for other stuff you likely already have something "native".

   Michal


