Jan Riis S�rensen wrote:

> Hi
> 
> I have just aquired this server and want to install Red Hat 7.2 for Alpha
> on this server. How do I access bios in order to change boot sequence and
> boot from drive A: or cdrom ??

The answer is - you Don't.

On swich-on it should either boot to a >>> prompt or to whatever is 
installed on the hard-drive 

If the hard-drive starts booting you will need to quickly press CTRL+C as 
soon as the 'CPU 0' message appears - this will return you to >>> prompt - 
alternatively boot machine with boot hard-drive (easy to find by watching 
drive LED's while booting) removed until >>> prompt appears then enter 'set 
bootdef_dev dka400'. When hard-drive is refitted it should then default to 
booting from CD - change the dka400 to dv0 to default to floppy.

To boot from the floppy drive enter 'boot dv0' at the >>> prompt.

To boot from CD you will need to enter 'show devices' and see which device 
listed is the CD Drive (usually dka400) then enter boot followed by device 
name - e.g. 'boot dka400'.

You shouldn't have any problems installing Redhat 7.2 on this machine as a 
commandline version but you may need to replace the onboard display card 
with a PCI displaycard to get the GUI working (I recently installed redhat 
7.2 on an alphaserver 1000 4/266 at work and had to fit an old ATI Mach32 
card to make the GUI work).

You may also have problems if you decide to use the hard-drives as a RAID 
array, if you let Redhat partition the drives it makes it hard to re-use 
them as RAID drives (it adds a BSD Header to the drives and refuses to 
remove it - RAID won't work with a BSD header) - when it lists drives to 
partition you will need to unselect any drives you want to add RAID to and 
partition them manually - My work machine has 1 4gb drive as '/' and 3x2gb 
drives with RAID0 data striping as '/usr' but you may have a better layout 
plan.


Hope this is of some help,
Nigel







