Hi Linux fans,

I'm having problems getting Redhat 7.2 running on an old alpha based server. 
It's an Alphaserver 1000 4/266 with 256MB Memory and 1x4gb and 1x8gb SCSI 
hard-drives. 

The company I work for was recently given this machine by a customer who was 
upgrading and as we didn't know their logon passwords we decided to try 
Redhat.

The problems we have are :-

The Graphical install won't work with onboard display card (Cirrus login 
CL542x chipset) - the display is corrupt.

We fitted a spare S3 Trio64 PCI card and installed ok - after configuring 
the card we now get error 'X Respawned too fast' on bootup. If we login to 
text console and run 'startx' the machine loads X then hangs while at a 
point that varies between half-way through loading KDE and just after KDE 
desktop finishes loading. 

Running xf86conf from commandline with S3 card fitted has same display 
corruption as when using the machine's own display chip but the display 
looks perfect when starting X (even when hung). 

If we run memtest at the alpha's bios commandline the machine flashes up a 
lot of text (too quick to read) then appears to reboot, starts counting 
then stops at number 4730 with error 'Serial Rom detected memory error'. We 
tried removing ALL memory SIMMS and putting memory from other banks into 
bank 0 with same result.

If we change logicboard link J1 to cache test positions we get 'Done' which 
I assume indicates test passed with no errors - we haven't had time to run 
full memory test yet (we left it testing main memory for an hour with no 
error messages though).

Would a BIOS reflash solve the problem or is something more serious wrong - 
we were hoping to use it to replace our department's Pentium Pro 200mhz so 
it's a shame if we have to dump this Alpha (especially if using it allows 
us to boast at having the only 64bit server in the company).

Thanks for any help offered,
Nigel.

P.S. I've been using Linux at home since 1994 (slackware 2.0) and I'm typing 
this on my current Mandrake 8.1 based AMD K6-2 500mhz - I've also persuaded 
work to install Linux on their department fileserver and several PC's that 
are used to test DAT-Drives using a custom bash-script that was originally 
written to run on SCO Unixware (we are a computer repair department).



