> - it's an XP1000
> - has 2 GB of RAM
> - the hard disk is brand new (18 GB, got to check the specs)
> - I also got to check what type of video card we have exactly.
> - etc... ?

Linux+SRM are a bit picky about the video card.  So yeah, that's going
to be a worry for you.  Also, I assume you're booting using SRM?
SRM is picky about what SCSI controller it can boot off of.  Check
that too, though you're probably all right.

>Compaq/HP offers RH 7.1 for only 29 bucks, so, we'll probably be
>buying it from them.  Does it make any difference?  Is Compaq
>adding things to make it work better?

I think they throw in a CD which "jumpstarts" you.  The only
gotcha I found in the RedHat install was that you have to read the
manual about partitioning very closely.  If you don't use the BSD-style
partitioning, you're not going to be able to boot your new Linux
install.  I didn't use their "jumpstart" CD, instead I used the
RedHat 7.1 CDs and read the manual closely abot partitioning.


Other than these gotchas, RedHat 7.1 runs fantastically well and
stably on our DS20E.  Add the compilers from uh, HP, and two years
ago we had a top-of-the-line machine...

Best of luck,

PeterM


